- who_they_are: Mostly beginner-to-intermediate DIYers (many describe themselves as having "no skills," per The Aesthetic Edge) prepping for a festival or summer event, searching close to the date, often doing this on their own without a nail tech.
- what_they_want_from_this_post: A tie-dye nail tutorial that actually works on the first try, with a technique that suits their patience level and materials on hand.
- what_frustrates_them: Existing tutorials assume the water-marble method works for everyone — reader comments across sources report polish sinking to the bottom of the cup instead of floating, polish drying/"skinning" before they can swirl it, and streaky uneven color when they don't use white base first.
- top_5_pain_points:
  1. Water marble technique failing — polish sinks instead of spreading on the water's surface
  2. Polish drying too fast to swirl before it sets ("formed a skin")
  3. Not knowing gel vs. regular polish changes the process (gel needs curing between layers; regular needs full dry time)
  4. Streaky/muddy results from skipping the white base coat
  5. Wanting a mess-free option because they're doing this at home/in a tent, not a salon, with limited cleanup tools
- exact_user_phrases: "why does my nail polish keep sinking to the bottom of the cup," "it wouldn't spread out," "the polish was drying faster than I could drop another drop," "I didn't have white for a base coat, so my colors aren't as pronounced," "deceptively simple," "no skills required," "a little bit of a mess, and a whole lot of color," "psychedelic," "groovy"
